nodecode-kokkai is a rewrite of TakeshiTGAL's TakeshiTGAL/hermes-plugin-kokkai:

  https://github.com/TakeshiTGAL/hermes-plugin-kokkai
  commit 0c9bcfc49f64fd063131bf2676904f2ea166002c
  MIT License, Copyright (c) 2026 TakeshiTGAL (LICENSE)

Rewritten: the plugin's three tools, reimplemented in Common Lisp against the
cell contract. `kokkai_search` asks the National Diet Library's meeting list
and answers one summary per meeting with the official meetingURL; the page
must hold every match or the call says so rather than reporting 0 meetings,
and a row without a speechID is not a page a watch may mark seen from.
`kokkai_speech` asks the speech search and answers the first 240 characters
of each speech, the speaker, the meeting, the date and the speechURL, with
the total and a truncation mark first. `kokkai_watch` saves a query, marks
what the window already holds as seen, and checks it daily: only the daily
check advances the seen speech IDs, so a manual run can be repeated. The
house set, the from/until pairing and order, the query-length ceiling, the
two-megabyte page cap and the five-second floor between requests are the
original's, as is the PDL 1.0 credit line each answer ends with, in Japanese,
naming the URLs that were used.

Left out: the Hermes `pre_tool_call` approval hook, since a Nodecode cell
writes its own state file instead of a config rule Hermes had to gate; the
`hermes cron` job and the `scripts/jp-kokkai-watch.py` script the plugin
wrote and left behind after uninstall, since the cell's own worker thread
checks the watches and nothing survives the folder but one JSON file; the
`deliver` target, whose platform names are Hermes gateway surfaces, replaced
by a standing Nodecode notice; and the plugin's "always" rule keys and its
unattended-session refusals, which belong to the other harness's approval
machinery.
